<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="54507" href="schedule/2012/spring/PSYC/437/54507.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2012" href="schedule/2012.xml">2012</calendarYear><term id="120121" href="schedule/2012/spring.xml">Spring 2012</term><subject id="PSYC" href="schedule/2012/spring/PSYC.xml">Psychology</subject><course id="437" href="schedule/2012/spring/PSYC/437.xml">Advanced Psychology Lab</course></parents><sectionNumber>SH </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>N-Way Component Analysis</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Introduction to three- and higher-way component models, the Parafac and Tucker models; hands-on practice with MATLAB for parameter estimation based on alternating least-squares (ALS) and use of constraints such as nonnegativity and orthogonality; application to practical problems; visualization of results. Students are expected to have basic understanding of matrix operations (e.g., inverse) and principal component analysis (PCA), and familiar with MATLAB for matrix manipulation and computational programming, or becoming so in the first 2 weeks.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2012-01-17Z</startDate><endDate>2012-05-02Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LAB">Laboratory</type><start>11:00 AM</start><end>12:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>R      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>219A</roomNumber><buildingName>Psychology Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Hong" firstName="S">Hong, S</instructor></instructors></meeting><meeting id="1"><type code="LAB">Laboratory</type><start>11:00 AM</start><end>12:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>T      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>819</roomNumber><buildingName>Psychology Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Hong" firstName="S">Hong, S</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>